---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
---

<DocShell
   title="CLI reference"
   summary="This is the command-family map for the shipped CLI. It is the fast answer to 'which layer am I supposed to use for this?'"
   links={docsLinks}
>
   <section class="ledger">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Command map</p>
         <h2>Command families</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag</code><span>Look up criteria, levels, search results, and coverage strategy.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>inspect</code><span>Explain which criteria look relevant for a page or Storybook story.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>drive</code><span>Run low-level screen reader actions through stable sessions.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>doctor</code><span>Report target availability and recording diagnostics.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>run</code><span>Execute <code>axe-core</code> or one named interaction pattern.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>verify</code><span>Turn evidence into criterion or level verdicts.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>mcp</code><span>Start the stdio MCP server.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>help-all</code><span>Print the entire help tree in one shot. This is the easiest starting point for agents.</span></div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">High-value cuts</p>
         <h2>Subcommands worth memorizing</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="mini-grid">
         <div class="stack">
            <h3>wcag</h3>
            <p><code>levels</code>, <code>criteria</code>, <code>show</code>, <code>search</code>, <code>coverage</code></p>
         </div>
         <div class="stack">
            <h3>inspect</h3>
            <p><code>applicable</code>, <code>criterion</code></p>
         </div>
         <div class="stack">
            <h3>drive</h3>
            <p><code>start</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>stop</code>, plus raw actions like <code>next</code>, <code>key</code>, <code>read</code>, and <code>logs</code></p>
         </div>
         <div class="stack">
            <h3>run</h3>
            <p><code>axe</code> and <code>pattern</code></p>
         </div>
         <div class="stack">
            <h3>verify</h3>
            <p><code>criterion</code> and <code>level</code></p>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Typical ladder</p>
            <h2>One criterion, end to end</h2>
         </div>
      <pre><code>node packages/cli/dist/cli.js wcag show 4.1.3 --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js inspect criterion 4.1.3 --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/status-message.html --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js run pattern status_message_probe --target virtual --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/status-message.html --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js verify criterion 4.1.3 --target virtual --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/status-message.html --json</code></pre>
      <p>
         That sequence is deliberate. Lookup first, scope second, evidence third, verdict last. Skipping
         straight to <code>verify</code> is how people end up overstating what the runtime proved.
      </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">shortcut</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            If you are still deciding what layer to use, go back to <a href="/workflows">workflows</a>.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Agent support</p>
         <h2>Print the whole command tree</h2>
      </div>
      <pre><code>node packages/cli/dist/cli.js help-all</code></pre>
      <p>
         This command dumps every shipped subcommand and flag in one pass. If an agent is exploring the
         CLI, this is better than crawling help one branch at a time.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Operating rule</p>
         <h2>Use the small tool first</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         If all you need is the criterion text, do not start a browser. If all you need is raw AT
         control, do not ask for a verification verdict. The CLI is split into layers so operators and
         agents do not blur those jobs together.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
